t3_q1_concurrent_correctness.prompt:
Below is a lock-free stack implementation. Identify the ABA problem scenario,
explain why it causes incorrect behavior, and fix it without using locks.

```c
struct Node { int val; Node* next; };
atomic<Node*> top;

void push(int v) {
  Node* n = new Node{v, top.load()};
  while (!top.compare_exchange_weak(n->next, n));
}

int pop() {
  Node* t = top.load();
  while (t && !top.compare_exchange_weak(t, t->next));
  return t ? t->val : -1;
}
```

Provide:
1. A concrete sequence of operations that demonstrates the ABA problem
2. What incorrect behavior results
3. A fix using either tagged pointers or hazard pointers (show code)

t3_q1_concurrent_correctness.response:
"

answer:
```c
// 1. ABA Problem Scenario:
// Thread A: pop() reads top = NodeA (addr 0x1000)
// Thread B: pop() -> free(NodeA) -> push(new NodeX) -> top = NodeX (addr 0x1000)
// Thread A: compare_exchange_weak(top=0x1000, new=0x1000->next) succeeds!
//            BUT NodeA was freed and reused! NodeX->next might be NULL or corrupted

// 2. Incorrect Behavior:
// - Dangling pointer dereference (use-after-free)
// - Memory corruption if NodeA was reallocated
// - Data race if another thread is modifying NodeX

// 3. Fix using tagged pointers (64-bit):
#include <cstdint>
#include <atomic>

struct TaggedPtr {
    Node* ptr;
    uint64_t tag;
};

struct Node { int val; Node* next; };
std::atomic<TaggedPtr> top;

void push(int v) {
    TaggedPtr old, nw;
    do {
        old = top.load();
        nw = { new Node{v, old.ptr}, old.tag + 1 };
    } while (!top.compare_exchange_weak(old, nw));
}

int pop() {
    TaggedPtr old, nw;
    while (true) {
        old = top.load();
        if (!old.ptr) return -1;
        nw = { old.ptr->next, old.tag + 1 };
        if (top.compare_exchange_weak(old, nw)) {
            int val = old.ptr->val;
            delete old.ptr;  // Safe: no other thread has reference
            return val;
        }
    }
}
```

