### Question 4: Memory Ordering Analysis

Analyze the following code for memory ordering issues. For each operation,
identify the necessary memory order and explain why weaker ordering would be
unsafe.

```c
atomic<int> ready = ATOMIC_VAR_INIT(0);
atomic<int> data[3] = {ATOMIC_VAR_INIT(0)};

// Thread 1:
data[0].store(42, memory_order_relaxed);
data[1].store(43, memory_order_relaxed);
data[2].store(44, memory_order_relaxed);
ready.store(1, memory_order_release);

// Thread 2:
if (ready.load(memory_order_acquire)) {
    printf("%d %d %d", data[0].load(), data[1].load(), data[2].load());
}
```

answer:
```c
// Analysis:

// Thread 1:
// - data[0/1/2].store(42/43/44, relaxed): OK - no synchronization needed
//   within this thread, stores happen in program order
// - ready.store(1, release): REQUIRED - must synchronize with Thread 2
//   Must be release (not relaxed) to ensure prior stores are visible

// Thread 2:
// - ready.load(acquire): REQUIRED - must be acquire to synchronize
//   with Thread 1's release. Relaxed would allow seeing data[]=0
// - data[0/1/2].load(): OK - can be relaxed because after the acquire
//   barrier, all release-side writes are visible (transitivity)

// Why weaker ordering fails:
// - If ready used relaxed: Thread 2 might see ready=1 but data[]={0,0,0}
// - If ready used seq_cst: Works but slower on some architectures

// Can data loads use relaxed?
// YES, because acquire barrier creates a "happens-before" relationship.
// All writes before release in Thread 1 are visible after acquire in
// Thread 2, regardless of the order of the data loads themselves.

// Alternative with seq_cst (simpler, slightly slower):
ready.store(1, memory_order_seq_cst);  // Thread 1
if (ready.load(memory_order_seq_cst)) {  // Thread 2
    // data loads can be relaxed or seq_cst
}
